{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAFvoK41TVbCECixMeAi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I changed the recovery middleware in `recovery.go` so it stays quiet when a handler panics with `http.ErrAbortHandler`. That error is Go's standard way to abort a handler on purpose, so it shouldn't produce a stack trace or a 500. Now the middleware just records the error with `c.Error` and aborts the context, the same way it already handles broken-pipe errors.\n\nI added `TestPanicWithAbortHandler` in `recovery_test.go`: it checks that the response status the handler set (204) is kept and that nothing gets logged. The gate passed: the test fails without the fix and passes with it.\n\nOne choice to review: I suppress the panic instead of re-panicking it. Re-panicking would let `net/http` close the connection, which is the usual meaning of `ErrAbortHandler`. I went with suppression because that's what the commit title asks for. I only ran the `TestPanic*` tests, not the full suite."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1321,"cache_read_input_tokens":34092,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1321,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":306,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a0e7201-6195-4163-9fc6-d3cc44053d83","uuid":"46987907-5644-4114-a3f2-435c1f813421","timestamp":"2026-10-18T19:31:04.404Z","request_id":"req_011CgAFvo7PcBAu8LgMansQy"}
